libsanitizer: do not re-enter libgcc's unwinder from the allocator

An ASan-instrumented binary hung before main() on mipsel, printing nothing,
with a single thread blocked forever on a futex. libgcc's DWARF unwinder is not
re-entrant: _Unwind_Find_FDE takes object_mutex and allocates under it, so
ASan's malloc interceptor -- which collects a stack trace -- re-enters the
unwinder from inside the locked region and deadlocks against the lock this same
thread holds.

Only mips is exposed. sanitizer_stacktrace.h sets SANITIZER_CAN_FAST_UNWIND to
0 for __mips__, so every allocation unwinds through _Unwind_Backtrace; arm
unwinds through EHABI, which takes no such lock, and elsewhere
fast_unwind_on_malloc keeps libgcc off the allocation path entirely.

Two changes. A thread-local guard in UnwindSlow, which is unconditional and
belongs to whichever tool is running. And a fix to __asan::ScopedUnwinding,
which guards the same recursion one layer up but cleared its flag on the way
out instead of restoring it -- so it stopped the first nested allocation and
let the second, of which libgcc makes several under that lock, straight
through.

Bisected with ASAN_OPTIONS=malloc_context_size: 2 or less runs, 3 or more
hangs, which is exactly the threshold at which GET_STACK_TRACE stops
short-circuiting and calls the unwinder.

Signed-off-by: OpenIPC AI team <ai@openipc.org>

--- a/libsanitizer/asan/asan_stack.cpp
+++ b/libsanitizer/asan/asan_stack.cpp
@@ -38,8 +38,11 @@
     }
   }
   ~ScopedUnwinding() {
+    // Restore rather than clear. libgcc's unwinder allocates more than once
+    // inside the region where it holds object_mutex, and clearing here let the
+    // second of those allocations unwind again and deadlock against the first.
     if (thread)
-      thread->setUnwinding(false);
+      thread->setUnwinding(!can_unwind);
   }
 
   bool CanUnwind() const { return can_unwind; }
--- a/libsanitizer/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_unwind_linux_libcdep.cpp
+++ b/libsanitizer/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_unwind_linux_libcdep.cpp
@@ -89,6 +89,37 @@
   return UNWIND_CONTINUE;
 }
 
+// libgcc's DWARF unwinder is not re-entrant. _Unwind_Find_FDE takes
+// object_mutex and allocates under it -- a btree node in gcc 12 and later, the
+// sorted FDE array before that -- so an allocator that collects a stack trace
+// re-enters the unwinder from inside the locked region and blocks on a mutex
+// this very thread already holds. A single-threaded deadlock, before main().
+//
+// It bites where the slow unwinder is on the allocation path AND the DWARF
+// unwinder is the one in use. On mips that is always: sanitizer_stacktrace.h
+// sets SANITIZER_CAN_FAST_UNWIND to 0 for __mips__, so every malloc unwinds
+// through _Unwind_Backtrace. arm escapes it by unwinding through EHABI, which
+// takes no such lock, and elsewhere fast_unwind_on_malloc keeps libgcc off the
+// allocation path entirely.
+//
+// __asan::ScopedUnwinding guards the same recursion one layer up, but only
+// when an AsanThread already exists. This guard is unconditional and belongs
+// to whichever tool is running.
+static THREADLOCAL bool in_unwind_slow;
+
+class ScopedSlowUnwinding {
+ public:
+  ScopedSlowUnwinding() : was_unwinding(in_unwind_slow) {
+    in_unwind_slow = true;
+  }
+  ~ScopedSlowUnwinding() { in_unwind_slow = was_unwinding; }
+
+  bool CanUnwind() const { return !was_unwinding; }
+
+ private:
+  const bool was_unwinding;
+};
+
 }  // namespace
 
 #if SANITIZER_ANDROID
@@ -125,6 +156,14 @@
 
 void BufferedStackTrace::UnwindSlow(uptr pc, u32 max_depth) {
   CHECK_GE(max_depth, 2);
+  ScopedSlowUnwinding unwind_scope;
+  if (!unwind_scope.CanUnwind()) {
+    // Called from inside libgcc's unwinder. Unwinding again would deadlock, so
+    // report the one frame that needs no unwinding at all.
+    size = 1;
+    trace_buffer[0] = pc;
+    return;
+  }
   size = 0;
   UnwindTraceArg arg = {this, Min(max_depth + 1, kStackTraceMax)};
   _Unwind_Backtrace(Unwind_Trace, &arg);
